WEBVTT

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:21.870
Cheedeb presents The Celebrated Jumping Frog of Calaveras County and

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:24.670
Curing a Cold by

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:40.850
Mark Twain. The California Gold Rush. On January 24th 1848

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:44.890
John Marshall discovered gold in the

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:51.870
American River at Sutter's Fort California. He wasn't looking for

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:55.780
gold. He discovered it by chance.

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:06.070
This discovery changed the destiny of California. Suddenly this wild

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.320
territory of the far west

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:18.990
became world famous. During 1849 more than 80,000 people

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.800
arrived in California looking for gold.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:29.650
They came from the United States, Europe, Central and South

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.420
America and China.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:42.980
They were called gold miners, gold prospectors or simply 49ers.

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:46.900
A lot of 49ers were poor.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:53.660
Their dream was to find gold and strike it rich.

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:59.610
A lot of them found gold but only a few became

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:05.970
very rich and important. With the hard-working gold miners

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:09.140
there came saloon keepers,

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:16.910
gamblers, dishonest people and outlaws. It was difficult to keep

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:19.620
law and order in the far west.

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:39.090
The Celebrated Jumping Frog of Calaveras County. A letter from

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:39.760
the east.

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:49.810
One day a friend of mine from the east wrote me a letter. He asked me to visit an old

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:51.850
man named Simon

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:58.890
Wheeler. My friend wanted me to ask Simon Wheeler about

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:00.140
a childhood companion.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:09.110
Leonidas W. Smiley. I found Simon Wheeler sleeping by the

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:11.850
stove of the old tavern in

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:21.790
Angel's mining camp. I noticed that he was fat. His

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.740
face was simple and peaceful.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:32.780
When he woke up I told him that a friend of mine wanted to know about a childhood companion named

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:40.750
Leonidas W. Smiley. Simon Wheeler blocked me with his chair

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:42.540
in a corner of the tavern.

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:48.640
Then very seriously he began to tell me the story that follows.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:54.190
There was once a fellow at the mining camp named

00:03:54.240 --> 00:04:01.050
Jim Smiley. It was the winter of 1849 or maybe

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:08.010
the spring of 1850. He was an unusual man. He

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:10.950
always bet money on anything that happened at

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:17.850
the mining camp and he usually won. Jim Smiley was

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:21.750
a very lucky man. When there was a horse race,

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:29.180
a dog fight, a cat fight, or a chicken fight he always bet on them.

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:39.500
Exercise four. Now listen and check your answers.

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:48.740
Dear Mark, do you still remember me? I'm William Brown,

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:50.860
your friend from New York.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:55.760
I want to go to California to look for gold.

00:04:57.100 --> 00:05:00.670
My dream is to live in a mining camp in the Sierra

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:06.750
Nevada mountains. Can you go to Angel's mining camp to

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:09.160
visit an old man named Simon Wheeler?

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:18.150
Simon knows my childhood friend Leonidas W. Smiley. I want

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:20.720
to know all about Leonidas Smiley

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:28.630
before I travel to California. Thank you Mark. Your friend

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:29.640
William.

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:40.410
Bets and races. Smiley owned an old horse that was

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:43.820
slow and sick but he won money on her.

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:51.150
The poor horse had asthma and other animal illnesses. At

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:58.330
races she had a head start because of her health problems. Near the end of every race

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:01.820
she became very excited and desperate.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:04.000
She galloped wildly.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:14.850
Kicked her legs in the air to the sides. Raised

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:17.820
the dust, coughed and blew her nose.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:23.480
At the end of the race she was always the happy winner.

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:32.230
Smiley also had a small bulldog named Andrew Jackson. Andrew

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:38.270
Jackson was a lazy dog and did nothing all day long but when Smiley bet money on

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:40.900
him he was at lively and ready to fight.

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:45.430
When there was an important dog fight at the mining

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:53.410
camp Andrew Jackson always grabbed the

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:57.610
captain between his strong teeth. He didn't let go until

00:06:57.660 --> 00:06:59.260
Smiley won the bet.

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:07.400
Life in the Mining Camps

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:14.450
From 1848 many mining camps were established in the gold

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:17.420
country near the American River

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:23.550
and in the Sierra Nevada mountains. These camps were small

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:26.630
settlements. They were organized

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:32.790
quickly to meet the miners every day needs. A mining

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:36.990
camp usually had a saloon, a hotel,

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:43.620
a stable, a general store, an undertaker, and a bank.

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:50.230
Miners often paid for their food, drink, clothing, and other

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:53.800
things with gold dust or gold nuggets.

00:07:55.420 --> 00:08:00.990
Brawls and fights were common and killings were not unusual.

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:07.440
These were tough times and camps were rough places.

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:14.380
Today a lot of camps have become ghost towns.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:21.150
Others have grown into towns and cities such as Angels

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:25.260
Camp, Auburn, Grass Valley,

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:34.980
Jackson, Mary Posa, Nevada City, Murphy's, Placerville, and Sonora.

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:40.270
You can visit them and their abandoned mines when you

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:44.490
go to California. Take U.S. State Highway

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:52.380
80 to Sacramento and then take California Highway 49 to the gold country.

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:57.740
Daniel Webster

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:04.730
Smiley found a frog one day and named it Daniel

00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:09.710
Webster. He took it home and planned to teach

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:17.740
it a lot of things. For three months Smiley sat outside his house and taught his frog

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:24.460
how to jump far. Daniel Webster was a clever frog and learned quickly.

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:29.670
Smiley gave him a little push from behind and Daniel

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:32.360
Webster flew into the air like a donut.

00:09:33.340 --> 00:09:36.780
He even turned one or two somersaults in the air.

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:41.520
Smiley taught his frog to catch flies.

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:45.500
Flies Daniel flies, Smiley shouted.

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:50.280
Daniel jumped up and caught the fly with his long tongue.

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:56.560
Daniel Webster was the champion jumping frog of Calaveras County.

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:04.320
Smiley won all bets with his handsome frog. He was very proud of Daniel Webster.

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:13.450
Do you have a pet frog? If you do, take

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:17.060
it to the Jumping Frog Jubilee.

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:26.500
Every year in May at Angels Camp, California, there is an exciting frog competition.

00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:34.250
It is called the Jumping Frog Jubilee. Frogs from far

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:36.420
and near can enter the competition.

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:41.760
The frog that can jump the greatest distance is the winner.

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:49.620
A frog named Rosie the Ribitter is now the jumping champion of the world.

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:57.410
In May 1986, she jumped 21 feet, 5 and 3

00:10:57.460 --> 00:11:00.770
quarter inches, about 7 meters.

00:11:00.820 --> 00:11:08.330
If you want more information, write to Calaveras Jumping Frog

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:12.860
Jubilee, Post Office Box 489,

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:20.180
Angels Camp, California 95222.

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:36.720
A stranger at Angels Camp.

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:44.320
Smiley kept his frog in a box with small holes in it.

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:52.080
One day, a stranger came to the mining camp. He saw Smiley holding the box.

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:55.660
What's in that box? As the stranger.

00:11:56.920 --> 00:12:02.100
Maybe a parrot, maybe a canary. No, it's a frog,

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:03.980
answered Smiley.

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:08.580
The stranger took the box and looked at it carefully.

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:13.420
Yes, it is. Well, what's he good for?

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:22.240
Well, Smiley said, he's good for one thing. He can out jump any frog in Calaveras County.

00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:28.070
Stranger took the box again. He looked at the frog

00:12:28.120 --> 00:12:32.010
for a long time. Then he returned it to

00:12:32.060 --> 00:12:36.400
Smiley and said, I don't see anything special about this frog.

00:12:37.180 --> 00:12:43.190
Maybe you don't, said Smiley. Maybe you understand frogs, and

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:45.360
maybe you don't understand them.

00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:51.180
But I'll bet $40 that he can out jump any frog in Calaveras County.

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:58.410
The fellow answered. Well, I'm only a stranger here, and

00:12:58.460 --> 00:13:01.040
I don't have a frog. I can't bet.

00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:09.230
That's all right. That's all right. Hold my box a minute. I'll go to the river and find a frog for

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:14.490
you, said Smiley. The stranger took the box and bet

00:13:14.540 --> 00:13:18.860
his $40. Smiley bet his $40, too,

00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:25.230
and went to the river. The stranger sat down and waited for Smiley.

00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:30.670
He sat there a long time, and he began to

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:36.150
think. Suddenly, he took Daniel Webster out of the box

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:44.120
and opened his mouth. With a teaspoon, he put some shotgun pellets into the frog's mouth.

00:13:44.980 --> 00:13:46.340
Then he put him on the floor.

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:57.550
Panning for gold. The expression panning for gold was born

00:13:57.600 --> 00:13:59.640
during the California gold rush

00:13:59.840 --> 00:14:06.210
in 1848. The miners used a pan with a wire

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:11.110
net on the bottom. They separated the gold from other

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:17.910
materials by washing them with water. Miners usually pan for

00:14:17.960 --> 00:14:19.760
gold in the water of the rivers.

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:25.620
The water of the river filled their pan with sand,

00:14:26.620 --> 00:14:32.080
dirt, stones, and perhaps gold dust or gold nuggets.

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:37.750
Other miners looked for gold in caves in the Sierra

00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:42.020
Nevada mountains, and they used a pickaxe.

00:14:43.380 --> 00:14:47.320
These caves often became gold mines.

00:14:50.180 --> 00:14:58.510
The big bet. After some time, Smiley returned holding a

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:00.020
frog and said,

00:15:00.880 --> 00:15:07.740
Now put him next to Daniel with his front paws in the same place. I'll give the word.

00:15:08.740 --> 00:15:19.070
Then, Smiley said, one, two, three, go. He and the

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:21.300
stranger pushed the frogs from behind.

00:15:22.180 --> 00:15:28.580
The new frog jumped forward, but Daniel didn't budge. He tried to move his shoulders,

00:15:29.130 --> 00:15:34.520
but he was anchored to the floor. Smiley was very surprised and unhappy.

00:15:36.040 --> 00:15:37.680
The stranger won the bet.

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:43.120
Well, he said, I don't see anything special about this frog.

00:15:43.820 --> 00:15:48.370
He took the money and left. Smiley looked at Daniel

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:52.060
for a long time. Finally, he said,

00:15:52.980 --> 00:15:58.930
what has happened to Daniel? He looks terribly fat. He

00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:01.520
caught Daniel by the back of his neck and lifted him.

00:16:02.360 --> 00:16:05.440
Cats alive! You weigh five pounds!

00:16:07.060 --> 00:16:13.700
He turned Daniel upside down, and the frog spit out a big number of shotgun pellets.

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:19.500
Now he understood what happened to Daniel. Smiley was furious.

00:16:20.480 --> 00:16:21.720
He looked for the stranger

00:16:22.150 --> 00:16:28.000
everywhere, but he never found him. Simon Wheeler's story ended here.

00:16:29.460 --> 00:16:36.280
I didn't really want to hear another story about Smiley's best, so I got up from my chair and left.

00:16:40.660 --> 00:16:47.360
Exercise three. Now listen and check your answers.

00:16:49.180 --> 00:16:54.580
Dear William, I was happy to receive your letter.

00:16:56.440 --> 00:17:00.960
I went to Angel's mining camp and found Simon Wheeler.

00:17:02.380 --> 00:17:05.430
He told me lots of things about your

00:17:05.480 --> 00:17:12.930
childhood companion, the Anita's W. Smiley. His first name now

00:17:12.980 --> 00:17:15.940
is Jim. He works as a miner,

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:19.620
and he bets on everything that happens at the camp.

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:23.820
He owns a frog that is a champion jumper.

00:17:24.980 --> 00:17:32.610
He usually wins every bet with his frog, but recently a stranger tricked him and he lost a

00:17:32.660 --> 00:17:40.500
$40 bet. When you come to California, I'll take you to Angel's saloon to meet the miners.

00:17:41.580 --> 00:17:50.560
Your friend, Mark, are you dressed like a gold miner?

00:17:53.220 --> 00:17:59.050
Looking for gold was not easy. It was hard work

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:10.460
from sunrise to sunset.

00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:16.780
Levi's Strauss was an immigrant tailor at one of the mining camps.

00:18:18.320 --> 00:18:24.320
He began making work pants called blue jeans or Levi's.

00:18:24.900 --> 00:18:28.780
Blue jeans became very popular in the west.

00:18:30.820 --> 00:18:35.750
Later on, Levi's Strauss opened a blue jeans factory in

00:18:35.800 --> 00:18:36.520
San Francisco.

00:18:37.400 --> 00:18:43.490
Today, in San Francisco, the Levi's Strauss offices are at

00:18:43.540 --> 00:18:46.480
Levi's Strauss Plaza by the bay.

